{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '65,90p;205,290p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '125,205p;360,475p;530,690p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc | sed -n '55,115p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,58p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '65,90p;205,290p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '125,205p;360,475p;530,690p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc | sed -n '55,115p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '1,58p'\"","aggregated_output":"    65\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    66\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    67\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    68\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    69\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    70\t  peer (3.3).\n    71\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    72\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    73\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    74\t  state).\n    75\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    83\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    84\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    85\tend.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n   205\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   206\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   207\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   208\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   209\t\n   210\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   211\t\n   212\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   213\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   214\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   215\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   216\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   217\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   218\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   219\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   220\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   221\t\n   222\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   223\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   224\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   225\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   226\t\n   227\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   228\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   229\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   230\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   231\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   232\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   233\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   234\t\n   235\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   236\t\n   237\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   238\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   239\t  different reachability.\n   240\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   241\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   242\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   243\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   244\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   245\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   246\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   247\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   248\t  yet.\n   249\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   250\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   251\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   252\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   253\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   254\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   255\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   256\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   257\t\n   258\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   259\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   260\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   262\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   263\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   264\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   265\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   266\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   267\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   268\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   269\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   270\t\n   271\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   272\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   273\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding\n   274\tids and reports each\n   275\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   276\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   277\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   278\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   279\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   280\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   281\t\n   282\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   283\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   284\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   285\t\n   286\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   287\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   288\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   289\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   290\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   125\t   carry (invalid-value) is answered with the oversize protocol error\n   126\t   in place of that element, the read not skipped and no cursor\n   127\t   advanced past it; any other refused write leaves the request\n   128\t   unanswered.\"\n   129\t  [chan-writer answer]\n   130\t  (let [r (stream/append! chan-writer answer)]\n   131\t    (when (= :dao.stream/invalid-value (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   132\t      (stream/append! chan-writer\n   133\t                      (error-answer (:dao.stream/identity answer)\n   134\t                                    (:dao.stream.remote/id answer)\n   135\t                                    :dao.stream.remote/oversize)))))\n   136\t\n   137\t\n   138\t(defn- answer!\n   139\t  \"The mirror's steps 1 to 4 for one well-formed request: look the\n   140\t   identity up in the table (not-found); check the op against the\n   141\t   entry's declared surface under the fixed mapping (no-surface);\n   142\t   answer descriptor from the entry's handle directly, adding the\n   143\t   declared surface, and apply cursor, next and append! through\n   144\t   apply-request with the channel context; append the answer with the\n   145\t   id and identity. For next with a budget k, and an ok first outcome,\n   146\t   the chase places the further outcomes under more.\"\n   147\t  [table chan-writer channel req]\n   148\t  (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity req)\n   149\t        id (:dao.stream.remote/id req)\n   150\t        op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\n   151\t        entry (get table identity)]\n   152\t    (if-not entry\n   153\t      (write-answer! chan-writer\n   154\t                     (error-answer identity id\n   155\t                                   :dao.stream.remote/not-found))\n   156\t      (let [h (:handle entry)]\n   157\t        (if (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\n   158\t          (write-answer!\n   159\t            chan-writer\n   160\t            (-> (stream/descriptor h)\n   161\t                (assoc :dao.stream.remote/id id\n   162\t                       :dao.stream/identity identity\n   163\t                       :dao.stream.remote/surface (:surface entry))))\n   164\t          (if-not (contains? (into #{} (:surface entry))\n   165\t                             (required-surface op))\n   166\t            (write-answer! chan-writer\n   167\t                           (error-answer identity id\n   168\t                                         :dao.stream.remote/no-surface))\n   169\t            (let [ctx {:dao.stream.remote/channel channel}\n   170\t                  outcome (middleware/apply-request h ctx req)\n   171\t                  more (when (and (= :dao.stream/next op)\n   172\t                                  (valid-budget? req)\n   173\t                                  (= :dao.stream/ok\n   174\t                                     (:dao.stream/outcome outcome)))\n   175\t                         (chase h ctx (:dao.stream.remote/budget req)\n   176\t                                outcome))\n   177\t                  answer (cond-> (assoc outcome\n   178\t                                        :dao.stream.remote/id id\n   179\t                                        :dao.stream/identity identity)\n   180\t                           (seq more) (assoc :dao.stream.remote/more\n   181\t                                             more))]\n   182\t              (write-answer! chan-writer answer))))))))\n   183\t\n   184\t\n   185\t(defn mirror-step\n   186\t  \"The pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer requests against\n   187\t   its table: `(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) ->\n   188\t   cursor'`. For each request read from chan-reader at `cursor`, in\n   189\t   order, the four steps of 2.3 run; the mirror holds no state between\n   190\t   calls beyond the returned cursor. A value that is neither a\n   191\t   well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as malformed\n   192\t   wire input. The step stops when the channel reader answers blocked\n   193\t   or end; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery cursor, the lost wire\n   194\t   values being lost. The mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites\n   195\t   a cursor, an anchor or an outcome: the served stream is the\n   196\t   original, its cursors, its positions, its gap, its end.\"\n   197\t  [table chan-reader cursor chan-writer]\n   198\t  (let [channel (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor chan-reader))]\n   199\t    (loop [cursor cursor]\n   200\t      (let [r (stream/next chan-reader cursor)]\n   201\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   202\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   203\t          (do (when (well-formed-request? (:dao.stream/value r))\n   204\t                (answer! table chan-writer channel\n   205\t                         (:dao.stream/value r)))\n   360\t   answer envelope added are removed, nothing else.\"\n   361\t  [ans]\n   362\t  (dissoc ans :dao.stream.remote/id :dao.stream/identity))\n   363\t\n   364\t\n   365\t(defn- translated\n   366\t  \"A filed protocol error at the reflection: transport-error with the\n   367\t   error's name as the reason. not-found marks the reflection gone at\n   368\t   filing; no-surface and oversize mark nothing.\"\n   369\t  [e]\n   370\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   371\t   :dao.stream.remote/reason e})\n   372\t\n   373\t\n   374\t(defn- learn!\n   375\t  \"The attach probe's ok: record the source's descriptor and declared\n   376\t   surface, plus :closable.\"\n   377\t  [refl answer]\n   378\t  (swap! refl assoc\n   379\t         :source-descriptor (:dao.stream/descriptor answer)\n   380\t         :surface (conj (into #{} (:dao.stream.remote/surface answer))\n   381\t                        :closable)))\n   382\t\n   383\t\n   384\t(defn- install-more!\n   385\t  \"Install each more outcome under the answer's served identity and at\n   386\t   the cursor that precedes it, so a later next at that cursor by a\n   387\t   reflection of the same stream returns the source's own outcome\n   388\t   without a request. The identity is half of the key: two served\n   389\t   identities can carry equal cursor values, and an outcome belongs to\n   390\t   its own stream.\"\n   391\t  [link answer]\n   392\t  (let [identity (:dao.stream/identity answer)]\n   393\t    (loop [cur (:dao.stream/cursor answer)\n   394\t           more (:dao.stream.remote/more answer)]\n   395\t      (when (and cur (seq more))\n   396\t        (let [[o & further] more]\n   397\t          (swap! link assoc-in [:filed-cursors [identity cur]] o)\n   398\t          (recur (:dao.stream/cursor o) further))))))\n   399\t\n   400\t\n   401\t(defn- absorb!\n   402\t  \"Consume one value read off the channel. A well-formed answer whose\n   403\t   id is outstanding and whose identity is the outstanding entry's\n   404\t   reflection's own identity is taken: a not-found error marks the\n   405\t   reflection gone; the attach probe's ok records the source's\n   406\t   descriptor and declared surface; an append!'s source outcome is\n   407\t   emitted on the event writer, correlated by id; any other answer is\n   408\t   filed under its id and its more outcomes installed. Answers whose\n   409\t   id is not outstanding, answers whose identity is not the\n   410\t   reflection's own -- a mismatch never completes a request, which\n   411\t   stays outstanding -- and values that are not well-formed answers,\n   412\t   are dropped as diagnostics.\"\n   413\t  [link v]\n   414\t  (when (well-formed-answer? v)\n   415\t    (let [id (:dao.stream.remote/id v)]\n   416\t      (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\n   417\t        (let [refl (:reflection entry)\n   418\t              req (:req entry)\n   419\t              op (:dao.stream.remote/op req)\n   420\t              e (:dao.stream.remote/error v)]\n   421\t          (when (= (:dao.stream/identity v) (:identity @refl))\n   422\t            (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id)\n   423\t            (swap! refl update :ids disj id)\n   424\t            (cond\n   425\t              (= :dao.stream.remote/not-found e)\n   426\t              (do (swap! refl assoc :gone? true)\n   427\t                  (when (or (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\n   428\t                            (= :dao.stream/append! op))\n   429\t                    (emit! link v)))\n   430\t\n   431\t              (= :dao.stream/descriptor op)\n   432\t              ;; The attach probe's confirmation. ok records; a\n   433\t              ;; no-surface or oversize error marks nothing.\n   434\t              (when (nil? e)\n   435\t                (learn! refl v)\n   436\t                (emit! link v))\n   437\t\n   438\t              (= :dao.stream/append! op)\n   439\t              (emit! link v)\n   440\t\n   441\t              :else\n   442\t              (do (swap! link assoc-in\n   443\t                         [:filed id] {:req req :ans v :reflection refl})\n   444\t                  (install-more! link v)))))))))\n   445\t\n   446\t\n   447\t(defn- channel-loss!\n   448\t  \"The channel reader answered end: the channel is gone. The link\n   449\t   abandons its outstanding ids, reporting each abandoned append! on\n   450\t   the event writer as append-unknown; filed answers are kept and\n   451\t   still returned; reattachment is the caller's policy.\"\n   452\t  [link]\n   453\t  (doseq [[id entry] (:outstanding @link)]\n   454\t    (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\n   455\t      (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\n   456\t                   :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   457\t                   :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\n   458\t    (swap! (:reflection entry) update :ids disj id))\n   459\t  (swap! link assoc :outstanding {} :channel-gone? true))\n   460\t\n   461\t\n   462\t(defn- retry-pending!\n   463\t  \"One more try of each probe the writer has refused, once per drain,\n   464\t   until a send is accepted; the accepted send is registered as the\n   465\t   outstanding probe and the normal filing path confirms it.\"\n   466\t  [link]\n   467\t  (doseq [[_ e] (:pending @link)]\n   468\t    (send-request! link (:reflection e) (:req e))))\n   469\t\n   470\t\n   471\t(defn- drain!\n   472\t  \"Read the link's channel reader to blocked, filing each answer: the\n   473\t   caller's own polling is the cadence and no driver step exists. An\n   474\t   end is the channel's loss; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery\n   475\t   cursor, the lost wire values being lost. Each drain is also one\n   530\t    (and (some? s) (not (contains? s (required-surface op))))))\n   531\t\n   532\t\n   533\t(defn- filed-answer-outcome\n   534\t  \"The outcome a filed answer yields: a protocol error translated per\n   535\t   the rules, otherwise the source's outcome map verbatim.\"\n   536\t  [ans]\n   537\t  (or (some-> (:dao.stream.remote/error ans) translated)\n   538\t      (bare-outcome ans)))\n   539\t\n   540\t\n   541\t(defn- filed-cursor\n   542\t  \"A filed answer for anchor `a` sent by `refl`, returned without\n   543\t   being forgotten: minting is idempotent, so the answer stays as true\n   544\t   as when it was minted.\"\n   545\t  [link refl a]\n   546\t  (when-some [[_ entry] (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)]\n   547\t    (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))\n   548\t\n   549\t\n   550\t(defn- filed-next!\n   551\t  \"A filed answer for cursor `c` sent by `refl`: the outcome an\n   552\t   installed more element put at `c` for the stream `refl` serves,\n   553\t   else the filed answer of a next request for `c`; returned and\n   554\t   forgotten.\"\n   555\t  [link refl c]\n   556\t  (let [k [(:identity @refl) c]]\n   557\t    (or (when-some [o (get (:filed-cursors @link) k)]\n   558\t          (swap! link update :filed-cursors dissoc k)\n   559\t          (filed-answer-outcome o))\n   560\t        (when-some [[id entry]\n   561\t                    (filed-for link refl :dao.stream/next c)]\n   562\t          (swap! link update :filed dissoc id)\n   563\t          (filed-answer-outcome (:ans entry))))))\n   564\t\n   565\t\n   566\t(defn- retry-read\n   567\t  \"The answer a just-sent cursor request gives: the source's map,\n   568\t   invalid-anchor included, arrives later as an answer.\"\n   569\t  []\n   570\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n   571\t   :dao.stream/retry? true})\n   572\t\n   573\t\n   574\t(defn- send-or-count!\n   575\t  \"Send the request for `op` with `arg` when none of this reflection's\n   576\t   own is outstanding; otherwise count one further ask of the\n   577\t   outstanding request, which re-sends it at the link's resend-after\n   578\t   k.\"\n   579\t  [link refl op arg]\n   580\t  (if-some [id (outstanding-for link refl op arg)]\n   581\t    (count-ask! link id)\n   582\t    (send-request! link refl (wire-request link refl op [arg]))))\n   583\t\n   584\t\n   585\t(defn- refl-cursor\n   586\t  \"The cursor operation: a filed answer for the anchor is returned,\n   587\t   and a filed protocol error translated; otherwise a cursor request\n   588\t   is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is transport-error\n   589\t   with :dao.stream/retry? true. Once closed, cursor answers closed;\n   590\t   once gone, transport-error naming not-found.\"\n   591\t  [refl a]\n   592\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\n   593\t    (drain! link)\n   594\t    (cond\n   595\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\n   596\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\n   597\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/cursor)\n   598\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\n   599\t      :else\n   600\t      (or (filed-cursor link refl a)\n   601\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\n   602\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/cursor a)\n   603\t            (retry-read))\n   604\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\n   605\t\n   606\t\n   607\t(defn- refl-next\n   608\t  \"The next operation: a filed answer for the cursor is returned and\n   609\t   forgotten; otherwise a next request is sent if none is outstanding,\n   610\t   and the answer is blocked -- nothing is observable at this position\n   611\t   through this handle yet. After close, filed outcomes then end. The\n   612\t   reflection never judges a cursor: cursor-mismatch and\n   613\t   invalid-cursor are only ever relayed from the source.\"\n   614\t  [refl c]\n   615\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\n   616\t    (drain! link)\n   617\t    (cond\n   618\t      (:closed? @refl)\n   619\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c) (result :dao.stream/end))\n   620\t\n   621\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\n   622\t\n   623\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/next)\n   624\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\n   625\t\n   626\t      :else\n   627\t      (or (filed-next! link refl c)\n   628\t          (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\n   629\t            (send-or-count! link refl :dao.stream/next c)\n   630\t            (result :dao.stream/blocked))\n   631\t          (translated :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone)))))\n   632\t\n   633\t\n   634\t(defn- refl-append\n   635\t  \"The append! operation: send the request and return the channel\n   636\t   writer's outcome -- ok is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly\n   637\t   the contract's Writing rule for a transport that carries values\n   638\t   toward a stream elsewhere. The source's outcome is emitted on the\n   639\t   event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; append! never\n   640\t   answers the source's full. Only an accepted send is in flight: a\n   641\t   refused append! never crossed, so its effect is not unknown.\"\n   642\t  [refl v]\n   643\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\n   644\t    (drain! link)\n   645\t    (cond\n   646\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\n   647\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\n   648\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/append!)\n   649\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\n   650\t      :else\n   651\t      (let [req (wire-request link refl :dao.stream/append! [v])\n   652\t            r (stream/append! (:writer @link) req)]\n   653\t        (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   654\t          (register! link refl req))\n   655\t        r))))\n   656\t\n   657\t\n   658\t(defn- refl-close\n   659\t  \"The close! operation, local: the reflection forgets its\n   660\t   outstanding ids and its kept, unsent probe, reporting each\n   661\t   outstanding append! on the event writer as append-unknown -- an\n   662\t   append whose answer never arrives has unknown effect; afterwards\n   663\t   append! answers closed, cursor answers closed, next answers filed\n   664\t   outcomes then end, and nothing crosses the wire. Idempotent, per\n   665\t   the contract's Close.\"\n   666\t  [refl]\n   667\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\n   668\t    (swap! link update :pending\n   669\t           (fn [kept]\n   670\t             (into {}\n   671\t                   (remove (fn [[_ e]]\n   672\t                             (= refl (:reflection e)))\n   673\t                           kept))))\n   674\t    (drain! link)\n   675\t    (when-not (:closed? @refl)\n   676\t      (doseq [id (:ids @refl)]\n   677\t        (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\n   678\t          (when (= :dao.stream/append! (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req entry)))\n   679\t            (emit! link {:dao.stream.remote/event\n   680\t                         :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   681\t                         :dao.stream.remote/id id}))\n   682\t          (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id)))\n   683\t      (swap! refl assoc :closed? true :ids #{}))\n   684\t    (result :dao.stream/ok)))\n   685\t\n   686\t\n   687\t(deftype ReflectionHandle\n   688\t  [state]\n   689\t\n   690\t  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n    55\t\n    56\t\n    57\t;; =============================================================================\n    58\t;; The pair reader: in's gap translated to end (3.3)\n    59\t;; =============================================================================\n    60\t\n    61\t(defn- pair-reader\n    62\t  \"A channel reader over `in`, a reflection: every `cursor` mints\n    63\t   afresh on `in` at `:newest`, ignoring the anchor asked -- the pair\n    64\t   is a live channel, not a history browse, and a reattachment's fresh\n    65\t   cursor is the same mint this makes on first use. `next` follows\n    66\t   `in`'s own cursor, an internal position no caller threads back\n    67\t   here; an `in` gap or end, and an `in` transport-error naming\n    68\t   not-found or channel-gone -- a reclaimed relay pair -- mark the\n    69\t   reader ended in `ended?`, shared with the caller so a retired pair\n    70\t   end can be told from a live one, and every further read, forever,\n    71\t   answers `:dao.stream/end` without asking `in` again; any other\n    72\t   transport error passes through, the binding not ended.\"\n    73\t  [in ended?]\n    74\t  (let [cursor (atom nil)\n    75\t        mint! (fn []\n    76\t                (let [r (stream/cursor in stream/anchor-newest)]\n    77\t                  (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n    78\t                    (reset! cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)))))]\n    79\t    (reify\n    80\t      stream/IDaoStreamReader\n    81\t\n    82\t      (cursor\n    83\t        [_ _anchor]\n    84\t        (if @ended?\n    85\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/end}\n    86\t          (do (mint!)\n    87\t              (if-some [c @cursor]\n    88\t                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/cursor c}\n    89\t                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    90\t                 :dao.stream/retry? true}))))\n    91\t\n    92\t      (next\n    93\t        [_ _c]\n    94\t        (if @ended?\n    95\t          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/end}\n    96\t          (do (when-not @cursor (mint!))\n    97\t              (if-not @cursor\n    98\t                {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/transport-error\n    99\t                 :dao.stream/retry? true}\n   100\t                (let [r (stream/next in @cursor)]\n   101\t                  (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   102\t                    :dao.stream/ok\n   103\t                    (do (reset! cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)) r)\n   104\t\n   105\t                    (:dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/end)\n   106\t                    (do (reset! ended? true)\n   107\t                        {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/end})\n   108\t\n   109\t                    :dao.stream/transport-error\n   110\t                    (if (contains? #{:dao.stream.remote/not-found\n   111\t                                     :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone}\n   112\t                                   (:dao.stream.remote/reason r))\n   113\t                      (do (reset! ended? true)\n   114\t                          {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/end})\n   115\t                      r)\n     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, deprecated whole when this lands (plan section 1).\n     8\tSlices and companion edits are in\n     9\t[the implementation plan][impl-plan].\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    12\t\n    13\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    14\t\n    15\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    16\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    17\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    18\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    19\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    20\t\n    21\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    22\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    23\t> client.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    26\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    27\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    28\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    29\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    30\tsentence).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    33\t\n    34\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    35\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    36\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    37\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    38\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    39\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    40\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    41\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    42\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    43\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    44\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    45\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    46\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    47\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    48\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    49\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    50\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    51\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    52\t\n    53\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    54\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    55\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n    56\tnotification; exactly-once delivery.\n    57\t\n    58\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
