{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '290,325p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc | sed -n '195,220p;300,360p;471,530p;634,656p'\"","aggregated_output":"   290\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   291\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   292\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   293\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   294\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   295\tthe event writer.\n   296\t\n   297\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   298\t\n   299\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   300\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   301\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   302\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   303\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   304\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   305\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   306\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   307\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   308\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   309\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   310\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   311\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   195\t   a cursor, an anchor or an outcome: the served stream is the\n   196\t   original, its cursors, its positions, its gap, its end.\"\n   197\t  [table chan-reader cursor chan-writer]\n   198\t  (let [channel (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor chan-reader))]\n   199\t    (loop [cursor cursor]\n   200\t      (let [r (stream/next chan-reader cursor)]\n   201\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   202\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   203\t          (do (when (well-formed-request? (:dao.stream/value r))\n   204\t                (answer! table chan-writer channel\n   205\t                         (:dao.stream/value r)))\n   206\t              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r)))\n   207\t          :dao.stream/blocked cursor\n   208\t          :dao.stream/end cursor\n   209\t          :dao.stream/gap (recur (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   210\t          cursor)))))\n   211\t\n   212\t\n   213\t;; =============================================================================\n   214\t;; The link (2.4, 2.5)\n   215\t;; =============================================================================\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn- new-link\n   218\t  \"One link per channel descriptor, shared by every reflection through\n   219\t   that channel. A link holds the channel writer; the channel reader\n   220\t   and the link's own cursor on it; the outstanding request ids with\n   300\t             {:req req :reflection refl}))\n   301\t    r))\n   302\t\n   303\t\n   304\t(defn- count-ask!\n   305\t  \"Count one further ask of the outstanding request `id`; on reaching\n   306\t   the link's resend-after k, re-send it once -- the same id, a\n   307\t   duplicate of an idempotent operation, which recomputes the same or a\n   308\t   later, equally true answer -- and start counting afresh. append! is\n   309\t   never re-sent: it never stays outstanding beyond its send.\"\n   310\t  [link id]\n   311\t  (when-some [entry (get (:outstanding @link) id)]\n   312\t    (let [k (:resend-after @link)\n   313\t          asks (inc (:asks entry))]\n   314\t      (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] asks)\n   315\t      (when (and (integer? k) (pos? k) (<= k asks))\n   316\t        (stream/append! (:writer @link) (:req entry))\n   317\t        (swap! link assoc-in [:outstanding id :asks] 0)))))\n   318\t\n   319\t\n   320\t(defn- outstanding-for\n   321\t  \"The id of the link's outstanding request for `op` with `arg` sent by\n   322\t   `refl`, if any; the request each outstanding id was sent for makes\n   323\t   the correlation.\"\n   324\t  [link refl op arg]\n   325\t  (when-some [[id _]\n   326\t              (first\n   327\t                (filter (fn [[_ e]]\n   328\t                          (let [r (:req e)]\n   329\t                            (and (= refl (:reflection e))\n   330\t                                 (= op (:dao.stream.remote/op r))\n   331\t                                 (= arg (first\n   332\t                                          (:dao.stream.remote/args r))))))\n   333\t                        (:outstanding @link)))]\n   334\t    id))\n   335\t\n   336\t\n   337\t(defn- filed-for\n   338\t  \"The first filed answer for `op` with `arg` sent by `refl`, in id\n   339\t   order, as [id entry]. A filed answer keeps the request it was sent\n   340\t   for and the reflection that sent it -- an answer belongs to the\n   341\t   operation that caused it -- so it is found whether or not its\n   342\t   outstanding id was abandoned.\"\n   343\t  [link refl op arg]\n   344\t  (let [filed (:filed @link)]\n   345\t    (when-some [id\n   346\t                (first\n   347\t                  (filter (fn [i]\n   348\t                            (let [e (get filed i)\n   349\t                                  r (:req e)]\n   350\t                              (and (= refl (:reflection e))\n   351\t                                   (= op (:dao.stream.remote/op r))\n   352\t                                   (= arg (first\n   353\t                                            (:dao.stream.remote/args r))))))\n   354\t                          (sort (keys filed))))]\n   355\t      [id (get filed id)])))\n   356\t\n   357\t\n   358\t(defn- bare-outcome\n   359\t  \"The source's own outcome map, verbatim: the correlation keys the\n   360\t   answer envelope added are removed, nothing else.\"\n   471\t(defn- drain!\n   472\t  \"Read the link's channel reader to blocked, filing each answer: the\n   473\t   caller's own polling is the cadence and no driver step exists. An\n   474\t   end is the channel's loss; a gap adopts the reader's own recovery\n   475\t   cursor, the lost wire values being lost. Each drain is also one\n   476\t   further ask of an outstanding descriptor probe -- the probe's\n   477\t   caller never asks it remotely -- so resend-after re-sends it, and\n   478\t   one more try of each probe the writer has refused, until a send\n   479\t   is accepted.\"\n   480\t  [link]\n   481\t  (when-not (:channel-gone? @link)\n   482\t    (doseq [[_ e] (:outstanding @link)]\n   483\t      (when (= :dao.stream/descriptor (:dao.stream.remote/op (:req e)))\n   484\t        (count-ask! link (:dao.stream.remote/id (:req e)))))\n   485\t    (retry-pending! link)\n   486\t    (loop []\n   487\t      (let [r (stream/next (:reader @link) (:cursor @link))]\n   488\t        (case (:dao.stream/outcome r)\n   489\t          :dao.stream/ok\n   490\t          (do (absorb! link (:dao.stream/value r))\n   491\t              (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n   492\t              (recur))\n   493\t          :dao.stream/blocked nil\n   494\t          :dao.stream/end (channel-loss! link)\n   495\t          :dao.stream/gap\n   496\t          (do (swap! link assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r)) nil)\n   497\t          nil)))))\n   498\t\n   499\t\n   500\t;; =============================================================================\n   501\t;; The reflection (2.4)\n   502\t;; =============================================================================\n   503\t\n   504\t(defn- result\n   505\t  [outcome]\n   506\t  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\n   507\t\n   508\t\n   509\t(defn- refl-descriptor\n   510\t  \"Local: ok with the remote descriptor the reflection was attached\n   511\t   with and the identity -- the same identity as the source's own\n   512\t   descriptor, different reachability. A name outlives what it named:\n   513\t   descriptor still answers ok once gone, once closed, and after\n   514\t   channel loss.\"\n   515\t  [refl]\n   516\t  (drain! (:link @refl))\n   517\t  {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   518\t   :dao.stream/descriptor (:descriptor @refl)\n   519\t   :dao.stream/identity (:identity @refl)})\n   520\t\n   521\t\n   522\t(defn- surface-lacks?\n   523\t  \"True when the source's declared surface is learned and lacks the\n   524\t   surface `op` needs. Before the probe's answer the reflection\n   525\t   declares #{:reader :writer :closable}, so every operation is\n   526\t   attempted; an operation the source turns out to lack is answered as\n   527\t   the no-surface case.\"\n   528\t  [refl op]\n   529\t  (let [s (:surface @refl)]\n   530\t    (and (some? s) (not (contains? s (required-surface op))))))\n   634\t(defn- refl-append\n   635\t  \"The append! operation: send the request and return the channel\n   636\t   writer's outcome -- ok is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly\n   637\t   the contract's Writing rule for a transport that carries values\n   638\t   toward a stream elsewhere. The source's outcome is emitted on the\n   639\t   event writer when it is filed, correlated by id; append! never\n   640\t   answers the source's full. Only an accepted send is in flight: a\n   641\t   refused append! never crossed, so its effect is not unknown.\"\n   642\t  [refl v]\n   643\t  (let [link (:link @refl)]\n   644\t    (drain! link)\n   645\t    (cond\n   646\t      (:closed? @refl) (result :dao.stream/closed)\n   647\t      (:gone? @refl) (translated :dao.stream.remote/not-found)\n   648\t      (surface-lacks? refl :dao.stream/append!)\n   649\t      (translated :dao.stream.remote/no-surface)\n   650\t      :else\n   651\t      (let [req (wire-request link refl :dao.stream/append! [v])\n   652\t            r (stream/append! (:writer @link) req)]\n   653\t        (when (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r))\n   654\t          (register! link refl req))\n   655\t        r))))\n   656\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-28 11:35:27 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 18:35:27 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n1. **The P1 is not a defect under the signed-off remote spec.** `mirror-step` advances its channel cursor after `answer!` regardless of the channel writer’s outcome ([remote.cljc:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:197), [remote.cljc:203](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:203)). That is intentional: the spec says the mirror keeps only its cursor ([dao.stream.remote.md:75](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:75)) and explicitly says there is no result retention at the answering peer ([dao.stream.remote.md:297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:297)). For `descriptor`, `cursor`, and `next`, the asker resends unanswered requests; they are idempotent ([dao.stream.remote.md:299](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:299), [remote.cljc:304](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:304)). For `append!`, the asker never resends; the source outcome is asynchronous, and an answer that never arrives has unknown effect, reported when the reflection closes or the channel ends ([dao.stream.remote.md:249](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:249), [dao.stream.remote.md:304](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:304), [remote.cljc:447](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:447)). Thus a refused answer can leave the outcome unknown until close or channel loss. That is a deliberate tradeoff, not an implementation defect. For the FFI case, the VM separately waits for its apply response; it does not need the remote `append!` result to resume.\n\n2. **No remote spec change is needed.** The export composition may retain apply server state and a response across a `full` append to the VM’s call-out stream; `apply/serve-once!` already defines this caller-owned retry behavior ([apply.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:217), [apply.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:301)). That state belongs to the FFI responder, not the remote mirror. Do not retain remote request answers to recover refused channel writes; that would contradict the signed-off spec.\n\n3. **Drop sub-slice 3.1.** The former mirror-retention slice is unnecessary. Start with the export binding: stable per-handle `serve!`, owned cursors and table, lease lifecycle, and apply responder state. Then add the real VM and UCF retained-call acceptance. Existing remote resend behavior remains covered by its own tests.\n\n4. **Sub-slice 3.2 can proceed with policy injected through options.** Supply the channel end, surfaces or handler gate, lease judge/holder wiring and required cadence/duration/tolerance, plus capacity and retention choices as composition inputs. `dao.lease/make-judge` requires cadence, tick/fact media, resolver, reclaim, and writer at assembly ([lease.cljc:2114](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc:2114)); those values need to be provided, but their deployment-specific settings need not be decided architecturally first. No owner decision blocks the export-binding implementation if it validates required options and refuses incomplete assembly. Handler authority remains an explicit injected policy: authentication/authorization are named non-goals of the remote spec ([dao.stream.remote.md:53](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md:53))."}}
